\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Linear block-term tensor regression (BTTR) and the block-term tensor network (BTTN). Top row: BTTR \cite  {Faes_2024}, labelled in the image with the online year of that article, splits the input tensor into blocks. Each block has two factor matrices, a core $\mathbf  {G}_k$ and a static score vector $\mathbf  {t}_k$. The blocks are fitted by deflation and give a linear score. Bottom row: BTTN takes the frequency by time by lead spectrogram tensor of one ECG. In each of $K = 12$ blocks it projects the tensor onto a frequency factor $\mathbf  {A}_k$ and a lead factor $\mathbf  {C}_k$, then filters along time with learned kernels $\Psi _k$ of three widths. In the reported model, the box labelled ``attention over blocks'' pools each channel along time by an attention-weighted sum and by its maximum. A nonlinear head maps the pooled values to a log-risk, and the network is trained end to end with a Cox survival objective. The clinical term is not drawn. It is a linear function of age, sex and ECG intervals, added to the head's log-risk without passing through the block-term layer. The risk curve is schematic; the text states the horizons at which the risk is evaluated.}}{10}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bttr-to-bttn}{{1}{10}{Linear block-term tensor regression (BTTR) and the block-term tensor network (BTTN). Top row: BTTR \cite {Faes_2024}, labelled in the image with the online year of that article, splits the input tensor into blocks. Each block has two factor matrices, a core $\mathbf {G}_k$ and a static score vector $\mathbf {t}_k$. The blocks are fitted by deflation and give a linear score. Bottom row: BTTN takes the frequency by time by lead spectrogram tensor of one ECG. In each of $K = 12$ blocks it projects the tensor onto a frequency factor $\mathbf {A}_k$ and a lead factor $\mathbf {C}_k$, then filters along time with learned kernels $\Psi _k$ of three widths. In the reported model, the box labelled ``attention over blocks'' pools each channel along time by an attention-weighted sum and by its maximum. A nonlinear head maps the pooled values to a log-risk, and the network is trained end to end with a Cox survival objective. The clinical term is not drawn. It is a linear function of age, sex and ECG intervals, added to the head's log-risk without passing through the block-term layer. The risk curve is schematic; the text states the horizons at which the risk is evaluated}{figure.1}{}}
